      "id": "p6-143-146",
      "part": 6,
      "label": "Questions 143–146",
      "passageTitle": "Article — Queensville Recycling Center",
      "passageHtml": "<div class=\"source-doc\" style=\"background:#fff;border:1px solid #c8bfb2;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 20px;line-height:1.62;color:#242424;overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><div style=\"border:1px solid #aca7a0;padding:14px 16px;margin:10px 0 18px;background:#fff\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px\">QUEENSVILLE (November 3)—Recycling just became easier for many local residents thanks to the opening of the township’s second recycling center. “West Queensville residents now have a more <strong style=\"white-space:nowrap;color:#984d06\">143. -------</strong> location to drop off their materials,” Mayor Dustin Larson said at yesterday’s ribbon-cutting ceremony. “No longer must they travel to the east part of the town.”</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 12px\"><strong style=\"white-space:nowrap;color:#984d06\">144. -------</strong>. However, Ida Aguirre of the Queensville Clean Coalition criticized the town council’s decision to eliminate curbside pickup of recyclables. “Curbside pickup should be resumed <strong style=\"white-space:nowrap;color:#984d06\">145. -------</strong> elected officials want to make recycling easier,” she said in a telephone interview. Open 6 A.M. to 8 P.M. on weekdays, the new 18 Darren Street facility takes only mixed paper and some plastics. Aluminum is not currently <strong style=\"white-space:nowrap;color:#984d06\">146. -------</strong>.</p></div></div>",
